    "updated_at": "2016-12-09T11:02:08Z",
    "body": "That still may not be enough. For example, `&#x0zzz` (corresponding to U+0000), or `&#xABCDEFzzz` (not corresponding to a Unicode code point), or even `&#xABCDEF;` (with the semicolon). Those don't fit the definition of \"hexadecimal numeric character reference\" and still nothing prevents them from occurring in valid text. It may be clearer to completely define \"character reference\" syntactically, before specifying the subset which constitutes valid character references.\r\n\r\nAnother approach is to expand the definition of \"ambiguous ampersand\", though that way we lose the distinction between bad ampersands parsed as text (\"ambiguous ampersand\" as currently defined) vs. bad ampersands parsed as references (missing semicolons, etc.). Conforming documents shouldn't care about this difference, but I'm not sure whether there aren't other places in the spec that depend on it.\r\n",
    "author_association": "NONE",
